derscore i'm sure there's somebody out  there that's screaming why hasn't he  said data table yet  um is it vroom vroom's super cool uh  those really great things with reading  csvs  or is it arrow's csv reading function  you might think it is since  that's your what i'm here to talk about  uh it turns out this is a trick question  because it depends on a lot of things  particularly when you're talking about  reading a csv  um you know your system uh features  matter  you know you might not have uh 5000 uh  cuda cores to on a gpu to read things  like wes does  um and so i could say that something's  fastest on my machine and you might find  something faster on your machine  also really important particularly with  parsing data files like this is the  shape of the data some  readers are better can better handle  certain sizes of data or shapes whether  it's  very many columns wide or lots of rows  or long  data types uh you know whether it's  strings or floating points or whatever  like these can all  different libraries have optimized for  different things  and so there's not generally an absolute  fastest  that said we can still we can still try  to make uh  make some sort of statement about uh  speed relative  across libraries so the only way we do  it is with benchmarking and benchmarking  you think of like as being like a  scientific experiment we're going to try  to hold as many of those variables  constant  so that except for the code that we're  running  to get our result and then we can  conclude that  at least within assuming those variables  the differences we're seeing is because  of  the code like a good experiment a good  benchmark  is explicit as to what you're holding  constant and what you're varying  and it's also reproducible  even so as with an experiment you have  this concern of external validity so  i can run something with on a certain  set of hardware  and with a certain data set and i might  be able to definitively say that under  those conditions  one is faster than the other but it  doesn't mean that that's going to be  globally true for all data sets and all  hardware so your mileage  will vary is an important caveat  so everything i'm about to show you and  in terms of numbers i'm going to show  you i will  of course say your mileage may vary  um vroom which i mentioned a second ago  is really awesome and um there's so many  things that i love about it  one of the things that i i love about it  today  is its benchmark suite it has this  vignette  that compares vroom to radar and  data table and the base read csv and  arena limb  and um and it compares against uh  multiple data files and kinds of data  including real data not just simulated  data that's a common  problem i think with benchmarks is that  you you're benchmarking something that's  not a real  real world scenario um and it does some  real work with it  part of the reason why vroom is fast is  because it defers the parsing  of the csv and so in order to  really compare head to head you have to  do some work with the  the data set because that's when you  actually pay for parsing it is when you  deal with it due to alt rep and some  other cool things it's got going there  this benchmark is is very uh this  report is really uh interesting in that  um  it's all very uh well documented and  scripted so you could  you could run it on your machine or uh  you could also  extend it by adding some more scripts so  that's what i did  i took rooms benchmarks and i added a  couple of variations so the first one  is just using the arrow csv reading  function and returning a data frame  and then where you can use data table or  dpi or whatever on top of that  the second version that i did  takes the arrow read csv function but  keeps the data in the arrow table format  doesn't bring it into r and does the  computation using the arrow  compute library on top of that which  takes advantage of some of those  features that wes was talking about  and finally um there's a number of other  of packages in the python ecosystem  that use arrow with some very  interesting results and there's a whole  project out of nvidia called rapids that  is a bunch of gpu  libraries they have a data frame library  called qdf  and uh so i called that from r  using reticulate and could and thanks to  some features in the error  package can pull results uh from qdf  off of the gpu into my r session  uh i've so i took the uh vroom benchmark  added these things to it and then i  published the report there the link is  there  should also say in case you missed it  the link to these slides is at the  bottom of every slide  and i tweeted it right before the talk  so uh if i'm if i if you've missed  something i want to go back it's there  i did use wes's very beefy nvidia  workstation that nvidia  donated to ursa labs to do this the  reason being  i wanted to test the gpu work and i  needed a machine with a nice gpu on it  so like i said there are a few different  data sets and  and things that it does it does this  basic workflow on all of them so you  know  it does the read csv but then it also  filters some rows does a group  aggregation  and stuff like that uh okay so  uh really quickly i want to show a  couple results if you want to look at  more again  the full set is on the is on my website  there  so the first uh benchmark in the suite  is  uh uses the a month of new york city  taxi data our favorite data set  uh 15 million rows as a csv  and so here's the results here uh  there's a lot of bars here so what i'm  going to do is i'm just going to i'm  going to gray out the ones that  are part of the core benchmark and just  focus on the  four that i added so that's arrow  reading  reading with arrow and then doing the  work with dplyr  reading arrow and then doing the  aggregation and stuff with data table  and then qdf on its own and then the  last one the fastest one down there at  the bottom  is the version using arrow and doing the  computation in arrow as you can see that  one comes out  to be fastest on this machine with this  beta  beating out uh broom and data table and  the rest there  and um interestingly the you can see  that the last bar there the last  segment of the tan one that says  aggregate that actually is the biggest  one  for the arrow version at the bottom and  that's because  we haven't implemented all of the  grouped aggregation uh stuff  in arrow yet so in order to do that  calculation it's actually pulling the  data into  r but even with that  uh all of the other work leading up to  that is still we're still feeding r very  quickly  um qdf in terms of the actual the  reading of the csv wasn't as fast but  the aggregations on it were super fast  100 milliseconds going over 15 15  million rows that was pretty cool to see  one other thing i wanted to point out  here is that these green bars  just show the time to read  uh an r data frame so the ones that are  all grayed out  completely don't actually yield a data  frame at the  end of the read csv command but these do  and what's interesting here is that even  just using arrow to read the csv and  getting a data frame  that's significantly faster on this data  set more than 10 seconds faster  than data table f read uh or all rep uh  i'm sorry vroom with without all rep  and um and so you could even just use  the arrow csv reader and then  not do anything else with arrow and you  can still get some significant speed ups  um really quickly just a couple of other  points you can see more of this in the  report  um i know i don't have the the slides  here but you can see them there  uh it and some of these other uh  variations  in the um in the vroom report  you can see the arrow really shines with  text data as  wes is pointing out the arrow's strength  in the format  in terms of non-numeric data and you  really see that when you're reading in a  bunch of big strings  r is not as efficient at that finally  uh the last benchmark that i'll show uh  there's also a multi-file uh taxi  data set example in the room benchmarks  using 12 months of taxi visa at csv  and i think this one's really  interesting so arrow's not the fastest  here  overall it's really fast at a lot of  things the aggregation the the reading  stuff is really fast  but there's this big one in the middle  that doesn't really register with the  other ones and that's the sampling  method  and that's because we haven't written a  give me 100 random rows from this data  set  method yet in arrow so i did something  kind of naive in r  just just so i could complete the  workflow here and illustrate that  and i think the point here is it's it  really showcases  the two things that i want you to take  away from this first is that  what eric can't do everything yet but  the things that it does do now  is really fast and can really help you  today so reading the csv itself  particularly when you have millions of  rows or lots of strings  that stuff is really fast now and it's  well implemented in arrow  but we haven't gotten everything yet but  i hope that gives you a sense that  uh the direction we're going and that um  when those things start to come online  and arrow you're going to continue to  see those  speed ups for you know that just happen  month on month  so we will hopefully in the future we  will go plaid thanks a lot everybody  thank you very much i just want to  remind weston neil to pop over to be  interviewed i love the spaceballs  references that was really well done  neil  much better than anything west did in  there i'm sorry i just had to give west  a little bit of trolling west you're  awesome too  but i seem to troll west a little bit  because that though as i say it's our  thing at least it's my thing  um and unfortunately i haven't seen you  in a long time so i haven't been able to  sneak our stickers onto your book bag or  your luggage or anything  hopefully you'll get to see everybody in  person soon
 

